## Statement

Assume the superstable regular model and Poisson conventions of [[thm-tdmath-continuum-variational-pressure]]. Let $D\subset[0,\infty)$ be an interval of positive length, let $\nu$ be the supremum of finite-entropy, finite-energy attainable densities, and set $e_0(\rho)=\inf\{\Phi(P):\rho(P)=\rho,\ I(P)<\infty\}$. If $\inf D<\nu$ and $e>\inf_{\rho\in D}e_0(\rho)$, define
$$A_L=\{N_{\Lambda_L}/L^d\in D,\ H_{L,\mathrm{per}}/L^d\leq e\}.$$
Then $A_L$ has positive Poisson probability for all sufficiently large $L$, and
$$\lim_{L\to\infty}L^{-d}\log Q_{\Lambda_L}(A_L) =-\min\{I(P):\rho(P)\in\overline D,\ \Phi(P)\leq e\}.$$
Let $R_{L,\omega}$ be the periodized empirical field of [[def-tdmath-periodized-empirical-field]]. There are $\beta\geq0$ and $z>0$ such that the empirical fields under the conditional law $Q_{\Lambda_L}(\cdot\mid A_L)$ and the matching periodic grand-canonical law concentrate in every open $\tau_L$ neighborhood of the common minimizer set of $I+\beta\Phi-\rho\log z$ when $\beta>0$. At $\beta=0$, retain the forbidden-pair set $B=\{x:\phi(x)=+\infty\}$ and use $I-\rho\log z$ on the hard-admissible stationary class $\mathcal K_B$, with grand weight $z^N\mathbf1_{\rm admissible}$; no value is assigned to $0\cdot(+\infty)$. If this minimizer is unique, both empirical fields converge to it in probability in $\tau_L$. These are empirical-field conclusions. The periodically continued conditional and grand laws also converge in $\tau_L$ under the same uniqueness assumption, for all measurable local observables of at-most-linear count growth. The energy constraint is an upper event and $D$ is nondegenerate; this does not assert exact $N$, a shrinking shell, or a singular energy surface.

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** A superstable regular pair potential, positive-beta finite-volume definitions or the stated retained-admissibility zero-beta continuation, a nondegenerate density interval and strict energy feasibility.

[F1] [[thm-tdmath-continuum-variational-pressure]]: $I,\Phi$ are lower semicontinuous on stationary laws, entropy levels are compact in $\tau_L$, and free/periodic pressures have the stationary variational formula.

[F2] [[thm-tdmath-local-tame-entropy-compactness]]: finite-entropy sublevels are compact and $I$ is lower semicontinuous in the local-tame topology.

[F3] [[lem-tdmath-closed-convex-separation]]: a closed convex subset of finite-dimensional Euclidean space can be separated from a point outside it.

[F4] [[thm-tdmath-specific-entropy-density]]: $I(P)$ is the large-cube limit of finite-region relative entropies against the fixed Poisson reference.

[F5] [[lem-tdmath-relative-entropy-variational-and-chain-rules]]: finite-region entropy obeys the product chain rule and is nonnegative.

[F6] [[def-tdmath-local-tame-topology]]: $\rho(P)$ is continuous in $\tau_L$, since it is a local observable with linear count growth.

[F7] [[def-tdmath-periodized-empirical-field]] and [[lem-tdmath-periodized-empirical-field-stationary-density]]: the event configurations have stationary periodized empirical fields $R_{L,\omega}$ with the stated local-tame tests.

[F8] [[lem-tdmath-periodic-law-local-tame-transfer]]: torus-invariant laws with bounded entropy density have relatively compact periodically continued laws and uniformly integrable local counts; empirical-field convergence transfers to those laws.

[F9] [[thm-tdmath-retained-hard-zero-beta-limit]]: the retained hard-admissibility partition has the variational minimum of $I-\rho\log z$ on $\mathcal K_B$; the local proof supplies the finite-original-energy separated-block approximation used to retain the exclusion at zero beta.

[L1] Georgii's Theorem 4 (1994, Section 7, equation (2.32) and probability formula (2.33)) states the common variational-minimizer and event-exponent result for a superstable regular pair potential, a nondegenerate density interval $D$ with $\inf D<\nu(\phi)$, and $e>\inf\varphi(D)$, using the periodic upper-energy event. Theorem 4 explicitly permits $\beta\geq0$ and asserts law accumulation/convergence, not empirical-field concentration. The complete proof in Section 7 was read; the local items provide the exact entropy, compactness and beta-zero convention interfaces.

## Proof

**Given:** The hypotheses and suppliers in Facts & Assumptions.

1.1 Define $m(\rho,e)=\inf\{I(P):\rho(P)=\rho,\Phi(P)\leq e\}$ and $s=-m$. For stationary finite-entropy laws $P,R$, the binary-mixture chain rule on a bounded cube $G$ gives $\theta D(P_G\Vert Q_G)+(1-\theta)D(R_G\Vert Q_G)=D((\theta P+(1-\theta)R)_G\Vert Q_G)+I(S;X_G)$, with $0\leq I(S;X_G)\leq H(S)<\infty$. Divide by $|G|$ and use [F4]; the label entropy vanishes, so $I$ is affine on finite mixtures. The expectation $\rho$ and finite-volume energy expectations are affine, hence their limits $\rho,\Phi$ are affine where finite. Mixtures of feasible laws show $m$ is convex and $s$ concave. If $(\rho_n,e_n)\to(\rho,e)$ and $\liminf m(\rho_n,e_n)<\infty$, choose near-minimizers with bounded $I$. Compactness [F2] gives a convergent subsequence; continuity of $\rho$ by [F6] and lower semicontinuity of $\Phi,I$ in [F1] show the limit is feasible with entropy no greater than the liminf. Thus $m$ is lower semicontinuous and $s$ upper semicontinuous; the same compactness argument attains every finite infimum. For strict feasibility, choose $\rho_1$ in the interior of $D$ below $\nu(\phi)$ with a finite-energy, finite-entropy law $R$. The inequality $e>\inf_{\rho\in D}e_0(\rho)$ gives $\rho_2\in D$ and a finite-entropy law $P$ with $\Phi(P)<e$. If $\rho_2$ is at an endpoint of $D$, mix $P$ with a sufficiently small positive amount of $R$; affinity of $\rho,\Phi,I$ keeps the energy below $e$ and moves the density into the interior of $D$. [F1, F2, F4, F5, F6, algebra]

2.1 For the lower event bound, take a feasible stationary law with strict density and energy inequalities. Approximate it by separated independent blocks: condition each block on a finite-energy, bounded-count event of probability tending to one, separate blocks by a fixed empty corridor, and average the phase. Its entropy per volume tends to $I(P)$; its empirical density and energy converge to $\rho(P)$ and $\Phi(P)$. The spatial likelihood theorem gives Poisson probability at least $\exp[-L^d(I(P)+o(1))]$ for the joint typical event, which lies in $A_L$. If a minimizing law lies on a constraint boundary, mix it with the strict feasible law. The mixture remains feasible for sufficiently small mixing weight and its entropy tends to the boundary law's entropy by the entropy affinity established in step 1.1 and lower semicontinuity. Thus the open strict event and the closed event have the same entropy infimum. [F1, F2, F4, step 1.1, algebra]

3.1 For the upper event bound, let $G_L=Q_{\Lambda_L}(\cdot\mid A_L)$. Its exact entropy is $D(G_L\Vert Q_{\Lambda_L})=-\log Q_{\Lambda_L}(A_L)$. Form the auxiliary stationary law by placing independent copies of $G_L$ in disjoint blocks, leaving a fixed empty corridor, and averaging the block phase. The independent-increment Poisson entropy chain rule bounds this auxiliary law's specific entropy by the displayed finite-volume entropy per volume. Its local limits preserve the closed density and energy constraints by lower semicontinuity of $I$ and $\Phi$, giving the rate upper bound by $m$. The matching lower bound follows from step 2.1. This auxiliary block law is used only for the event exponent. [F1, F2, F5, F6, L1, step 2.1, algebra]

4.1 The tangent-plane argument in [L1] separates the strict-feasibility constraint set in finite dimension [F3]. The objective coefficient is nonzero by strict feasibility, so it can be normalized to one; this does not make the energy coefficient strictly positive. Since $s$ is nondecreasing in the energy allowance, its tangent energy coefficient is $\beta\geq0$, with finite $\alpha=\log z$. Every constrained entropy minimizer then minimizes $I+\beta\Phi-\alpha\rho$ at positive beta. At zero beta the finite-energy feasible laws are hard-admissible; the retained-admissibility extension and separated-block approximation identify the corresponding minimum on $\mathcal K_B$. The finite-volume hard indicator is retained throughout. Theorem 4 and Proposition 7.3 give the conditional-law accumulation conclusion. Proposition 7.4 proves the positive-beta grand conclusion; the zero-beta grand conclusion follows from the retained-admissibility entropy proof in [F9], rather than an extension of that proposition's strictly positive-beta hypothesis. [F1, F3, F9, L1, step 1.1, step 3.1, algebra]

5.1 For completeness, the empirical concentration is the extension discussed after Theorem 4, not an additional assertion of its printed formula. The Poisson empirical-field upper bound used in [L1], Lemma 7.2 equation (7.4), applies to every closed subset of the constraint set. Outside an open neighborhood of the constrained entropy minimizers its entropy infimum is strictly above the constrained minimum: otherwise compact entropy levels yield a minimizing limit outside that neighborhood. Divide this exponentially smaller numerator by the event probability from steps 2.1–3.1. Thus the conditional empirical field concentrates on the constrained minimizers, hence on their containing grand minimizer set. For positive beta, the grand empirical-field conclusion is the good large-deviation principle of [L1], Theorem 2. For zero beta, tilt Poisson measure by $z^N$ and restrict to the closed hard-admissible class. Its upper bound has rate $I-\alpha\rho$; the separated-block lower bound gives its partition minimum. Compact sublevels follow from $I\geq a\rho-(e^a-1)$ with $a>\max(\alpha,0)$, so the same strict-gap argument gives grand concentration. A singleton grand minimizer therefore gives empirical-field convergence in probability in both branches. [F1, F2, F5, F6, F7, F9, L1, step 2.1, step 3.1, step 4.1, algebra]

6.1 The conditional law is torus-invariant and its entropy density is $-L^{-d}\log Q_L(A_L)$, which is bounded by the event limit. The matching grand law is torus-invariant as well. At positive beta its entropy density is bounded by the pressure identity, the superstable lower energy bound, and the Poisson entropy count inequality; at zero beta use the same identity with the hard indicator and omit the energy term. Consequently [F8] applies. Its transfer result converts step 5.1 into convergence of the periodically continued laws for every local tame observable under uniqueness. No zero extension outside the fundamental cube is used. [F1, F5, F8, step 3.1, step 5.1, algebra] ∎
